- contour-iterator-dangling-pointer: FIXED. js_contour_iterator()
  (js_contour.cpp:1657-1664) built a PointIterator from raw pointers
  s->data() / s->data()+s->size() into the std::vector<JSPointData<double>>
  backing a Contour, but never JS_DupValue()'d the Contour to keep it (and
  its backing vector) alive, and had no way to detect vector reallocation.
  Fixed by adding an `owner` JSValueConst parameter to js_point_iterator_new()
  (js_point_iterator.hpp/.cpp) that is dup'd into a new `obj` field on
  JSPointIteratorData and freed in the finalizer; js_contour_iterator() now
  passes `this_val` as the owner.

    let c = new cv.Contour([[0,0],[1,1],[2,2]]);
    let it = c.points();
    c.push([3,3]); // may reallocate the backing vector
    it.next(); // used to dereference a dangling pointer

- contour-intersect-empty-underflow: FIXED. contour_intersect()
  (js_contour.hpp:87-121) computed `aend = ita + a.size() - 1` /
  `bend = itb + b.size() - 1`. size() is size_t, so when either contour is
  empty this underflowed to SIZE_MAX, producing a wild pointer passed into
  std::find_first_of. Fixed by returning false immediately when either
  contour is empty.

    cv.Contour.intersect(new cv.Contour([]), new cv.Contour([[0,0],[1,1]]));

- mat-set-argv-overread: FIXED. js_mat_set() (js_mat.cpp:1069-1132) was
  registered with declared arity 2 (JS_CFUNC_DEF("set", 2, js_mat_set)),
  but optionally advances argv/decrements argc once for a leading Point
  arg, or twice for separate row/col args, then unconditionally read
  argv[0] again as the value to store. QuickJS only pads argv up to the
  declared arity when the real call passes fewer args than that; a call
  matching the declared arity (2) left no real third slot, so the final
  argv[0] read was out-of-bounds relative to the actual argument buffer.
  Fixed by bumping the declared arity to 3 (so QuickJS guarantees a padded,
  safely-readable third slot) and adding an explicit argc check that throws
  a TypeError when no value argument remains.

    let m = new cv.Mat(2, 2, cv.CV_8UC1);
    m.set(0, 0); // row/col consumed both declared args; value read was OOB

- js-is-typedarray-refleak: FIXED. js_is_typedarray()
  (include/jsbindings.hpp:545-551) called js_typedarray_constructor(ctx)
  twice - once assigned to `ctor` and freed, and once passed directly as
  the second argument to JS_IsInstanceOf(). The second call's returned
  reference was never freed, leaking one reference every time this
  function ran. It sits on the hot path of js_is_array(), so essentially
  any argument type-checked as a possible TypedArray leaked a reference.
  Fixed by reusing the already-dup'd `ctor` value for both calls.

    // any binding call whose argument is checked with js_is_array()/
    // js_is_typedarray() used to leak a JSValue ref each time, e.g.:
    cv.add(new Float64Array([1,2,3]), new Float64Array([1,2,3]));

- js-function-name-uninitialized-read: FIXED. js_function_name()
  (include/jsbindings.hpp:890-916) declared `const char *str, *name;`
  uninitialized, and only assigned `name` inside the branch where
  JS_ToCString succeeded AND the string started with "function ". The
  subsequent `if(!name)` check read `name` uninitialized whenever
  JS_ToCString failed or the stringified value didn't start with that
  literal (e.g. arrow functions, bound functions, classes) - undefined
  behavior that could branch either way depending on stack garbage.
  Reachable via js_object_classname() (jsbindings.hpp:298-308). Fixed by
  initializing `name = nullptr`.

    // any path that stringifies a non-"function "-prefixed callable via
    // js_function_name(), e.g. passing an arrow function where a class/
    // constructor name is introspected.

- contour-intersect-not-a-bug: retracted. Earlier entries here claimed
  `Contour.prototype.intersect` was unreachable/broken because
  `typeof contour.intersect` is `undefined` and "fixed" it to read the
  first contour from `this_val`. That was wrong: `intersect`
  (js_contour.cpp:1731) is registered in `js_contour_static_funcs`, not
  `js_contour_proto_funcs` - it is a *static* method by design, called as
  `cv.Contour.intersect(a, b)`, exactly matching the original
  argv[0]/argv[1] implementation. Reverted the this_val change and the
  arity-1 change back to the original argv[0]/argv[1]/arity-2 form.
  contour-intersect-empty-underflow above (the actual bug, now fixed) is
  the only real defect that was here.

    cv.Contour.intersect(c1, c2); // correct usage - static, not instance

- qjs-swallows-uncaught-exceptions-with-native-module: NOT a qjs-opencv
  bug - root cause lives in the `qjs` CLI / QuickJS engine itself (a
  separate project, not this repo), recorded here only because it was
  discovered while chasing the contour-intersect-ignores-this repro above
  (an unrelated-looking "process exits silently" symptom). Confirmed the
  opencv module is not implicated: `import * as std from 'std'; gc();`
  (no opencv involved at all) reproduces the same silent exit(0) with no
  error printed, whereas the identical script with no import correctly
  prints the exception and exits 1. So this is a bug in how `qjs` reports
  uncaught top-level exceptions once *any* native module has been
  dynamically loaded via the module system - out of scope to fix from
  qjs-opencv. Worth reporting upstream / fixing in the quickjs project
  directly if it matters (it currently means any qjs-opencv script's
  uncaught errors are invisible).

    import * as std from 'std';
    console.log("before");
    gc(); // or any other uncaught exception
    console.log("after"); // never reached, and no error is printed either;
                           // process exits 0. Without the import, the
                           // same script prints the ReferenceError and
                           // exits 1 as expected.
